  "content": {
    "id": "p30-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Suppose $f:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ where $f(x)=\\log(x)-\\log(x+1)$. Show that $f$ is strictly increasing.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "For $0<a<b$,\n$$f(b)-f(a)=\\log\\left(1+\\frac{b-a}{a(b+1)}\\right)>0.$$\nHence $f$ is strictly increasing.",
      "worked": "Let $0<a<b$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(b)-f(a)&=\\log b-\\log(b+1)-\\log a+\\log(a+1)\\\\f(b)-f(a)&=\\log\\left(\\frac{b(a+1)}{a(b+1)}\\right)\\\\f(b)-f(a)&=\\log\\left(\\frac{ab+b}{ab+a}\\right)\\\\f(b)-f(a)&=\\log\\left(1+\\frac{b-a}{ab+a}\\right)\\end{align*}$$\nSince $b-a>0$ and $ab+a>0$, the logarithm argument exceeds $1$. Therefore $f(b)-f(a)>0$, so $f(a)<f(b)$. Hence $f$ is strictly increasing on $(0,\\infty)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 50
  },
